// Heart: what the regulars say as they get to know you, and the radio between calls. Plain everyday speech.
// HEART[id] = [stranger, knows your name, friend, best friend]. Each line unlocks once with the friendship and then
// comes back now and then. The last line is the thing they don't tell people. Funny first, honest later, like people.

export const HEART = {
  delgado: ['Hey, you want a hot dog? Mustard\'s on the left, ketchup\'s on the right. Or you\'re just looking, that\'s fine too.',
    'There he is! Same as last time, right? I already started it, don\'t worry about it.',
    'My middle daughter\'s doing her residency now. Pediatrics. She calls me every Sunday to make sure I\'m sitting down enough. I\'m sitting right now, see? I\'m sitting.',
    'You want to know why I never take a day off? My first week out here, I watched a cop buy two hot dogs and leave one next to a guy sleeping on that bench. He didn\'t wake him up or nothing, he just left it. And I thought, okay, this is a good place. I want to be here in case I get to see that again.'],
  pruitt: ['Careful, don\'t step on that gray one. That\'s Gerald. He\'s slow and he holds a grudge.',
    'You\'re walking it better now. You\'ve got your head up. Took me about eight years to stop staring at my shoes.',
    'My wife used to make me empty my pockets on the porch before I came in the house. She didn\'t want the job coming inside. She\'s been gone six years now and I still do it every night. I don\'t really know why.',
    'March of \'88 I had a call with a little kid. I did everything right and it didn\'t matter, he didn\'t make it. The next morning I came out here and started feeding these birds because I didn\'t know what else to do with myself. That was thirty-some years ago. You don\'t get over some calls. You just learn to live around them.'],
  dot: ['Sit wherever you want, hon, just not booth four. Menu\'s up on the wall.',
    'Cream and two sugars, right? I told you I\'d figure you out. I\'m never wrong about coffee.',
    'The people who come in here at three in the morning are either working, or they just lost somebody, or they\'re in love. Sometimes all three. I just try to keep their coffee hot.',
    'Henry always tipped me in quarters. He said dollars were for the rent and quarters were for the jukebox. I was holding his coffee cup when I heard it on the scanner. I didn\'t put it down for an hour. I still pour him one every night. I know it\'s silly. Please don\'t tell me it\'s silly.'],
  marcus: ['Hey, real quick, don\'t trust the elevator in the Meridian on six. Take the stairs. Okay, I gotta go.',
    'Man, you\'re everywhere today! Every corner I turn, there you are.',
    'Everybody thinks bike messengers are crazy. I\'ve been doing this eleven years and I\'ve never hit anybody. I\'m careful. I just look fast because everybody else is standing still.',
    'Honestly? I ride because when I stop, I start thinking. Some nights after work I just ride the whole downtown loop until I\'m tired enough to sleep. I don\'t know. You probably get that.'],
  okafor: ['If you\'re here for the Chapter 5 book, all three copies are checked out. Restrooms are past the atlases.',
    'I put a book aside for you. I know you didn\'t ask. That\'s kind of what librarians do.',
    'My mother taught forty kids with nine books back home. She had them take turns reading out loud so the books would last. I think about her every time somebody complains the Wi-Fi is slow.',
    'When my family came here I was eleven, and I didn\'t talk like the other kids. The librarian at the old branch let me stay until closing every day and she never once asked me why. I never got to thank her. So I guess this whole job is me thanking her.'],
  kevin: ['Is it the terminal or the radio? Either way, try turning it off and back on first. Seriously.',
    'Hey, you\'re the one who actually restarts things before calling me! You might be my favorite person in this building.',
    'Nobody notices IT until something breaks. So if I\'m doing my job right, nobody knows I\'m here. Some days that\'s kind of lonely, honestly.',
    'The old server in the basement still has the very first dispatch log on it. February 1993. The first thing Velvet ever typed was "Good morning, Port Meridian." Sometimes when the building\'s empty I pull it up and read it. I don\'t know why. I just like knowing how it all started.'],
  rosa: ['You need something, or you just looking around? Take your time.',
    'Here, take a water. No, take it, you look like you\'re melting. Pay me whenever.',
    'That notebook behind the register is everybody who couldn\'t pay me back in 2008. I never crossed anybody out. I don\'t want the money. I just want to remember who stayed in this neighborhood when things got bad. Every single name in there still shops here.',
    'I almost closed the store in 2009. I had the sign written and everything. Then Mrs. Paredes walked in with an envelope with nineteen dollars in it from the whole block. She said it was for the light bill. Nineteen dollars. I went in the walk-in cooler and cried so nobody would see me.'],
  earl: ['What\'s it doing? Just tell me what it\'s doing. You don\'t have to make the noise, everybody makes the noise.',
    'Your brakes are squeaking, I can hear it from here. Bring it by and I\'ll take a look.',
    'See that handprint in the concrete? That\'s my dad\'s. He poured that floor in \'79. Every morning I stand right next to it. We wore the same size boots.',
    'During the \'83 storm my dad fixed every patrol car in the county for free. Everybody thinks he did it for the cops. My mom was a nurse at County, and the cops were the only ones who could get her to work. He never told anybody that. He did it because he loved her.'],
  reyes: ['You here to train or just watch? Either way, there\'s water in the cooler.',
    'I bet your guard drops when you get tired. Everybody\'s does. Mine still does, and I\'m fifty-one.',
    'Kids come in here mad at everything. I don\'t tell them to stop being mad. I just show them the bag and tell them to hit that instead of somebody.',
    'I lost a kid once. Tomás. Sixteen, best jab I ever saw. One March he just stopped coming and I didn\'t go looking for him. I figured he\'d come back. He didn\'t. Now if anybody misses a week, I go knock on their door. Every time. I don\'t care if it\'s embarrassing.'],
  kim: ['Machines eight and eleven eat quarters, there\'s a sign. Nobody reads the sign.',
    'You fold your clothes really neat. My wife used to say you can trust people who fold neat.',
    'The big dryer at the end, I named her Min-ji, after my wife. Twelve years and she\'s never broken down once. My son says that\'s just a coincidence.',
    'I sold my wife\'s piano to buy this place. She used to play every night. Now when I close up, I sit next to Min-ji until the last load finishes. I don\'t know. It\'s just nice to sit with her a while.'],
  ruth: ['Slow down, young man, you\'re walking past my porch. I\'ve been watching this street since before you were born.',
    'You went back in service without telling dispatch the other night. I heard it. I\'ve got a scanner and nothing but time.',
    'I worked the sheriff\'s switchboard at night for twenty-eight years. I talked a lot of deputies home through things they never told their wives about. The good ones still call me on my birthday.',
    'One night in 1974 a deputy keyed up his radio and didn\'t say anything. He was just breathing. So I kept talking to him. The weather, baseball, my tomatoes, anything. Forty minutes. Then he said, "Thank you, Ruthie," and got back in his car. I never told anybody that. I\'m telling you because one of these days you\'ll be on one end of that or the other.'],
  dez: ['I\'m not doing anything, I\'m just sitting on my board. That\'s legal. I looked it up.',
    'Oh, it\'s you. Okay, you\'re cool. Mostly.',
    'When I was twelve a cop caught me tagging a wall, and instead of writing me up he gave me this skateboard. Told me if I wanted to leave my mark on the city, do it with wheels. Corny, right? But it worked.',
    'My brother got caught at the same wall two years before me. Different cop. He got a record. He\'s thirty now and he still can\'t get a decent job. Same wall, same age, totally different life. So, like, be the skateboard cop. Please.'],
  hal: ['Slips are full, the ramp closes at dark, and you wear a life jacket. That\'s about it.',
    'Wind\'s turning southeast. You can\'t feel it yet, but you will.',
    'I ran charters on this river for forty years. It\'ll give you the best day of your life, and then the next week it\'ll try to drown somebody you love. You respect it or you stay off it.',
    'People always ask me about the plaque with the eleven names. I don\'t think about those eleven much. I think about the one who isn\'t on it. A boy in \'91. I had him by the wrist, and then I didn\'t. Every year on that day I go out to that spot and drop a line in. No bait. I\'m not trying to catch anything.'],
  bobby: ['Shrimp\'s live, crickets are in the back, and the coffee\'s from this morning. Sorry about the coffee.',
    'You came back! Most people just come in to use the bathroom. Here, want a hat?',
    'My wife Rose painted that old gas pump to look like a fish. She said if it had to sit there doing nothing, it might as well look happy. She was a lot funnier than me.',
    'Rose ran this shop her whole last summer from that lawn chair right there. Chemo on Mondays, and she was back in that chair on Tuesday. She sold more bait sitting down than I ever did standing up. I haven\'t moved the chair. People think it\'s for customers. It\'s not.'],
  johnson: ['Wait for my sign, baby. I don\'t care if you\'re police.',
    'You always stop for my kids in the morning. I notice that. Thank you.',
    'I took this job the year my son started kindergarten across this street. I couldn\'t stand the thought of him crossing without me. He\'s thirty-four now and a firefighter, and I\'m still out here. Nobody ever told me to stop.',
    'Every morning on his way to the station he honks twice short, once long. That\'s meant "I love you, Mama" since he was sixteen. With his job, and yours, I know someday I might not hear it. I try not to think about that too much. But I always listen for it.'],
  miguel: ['Come on in, the doors are open. The coffee\'s not great, but it\'s hot.',
    'You look tired. I\'m not trying to preach at you. Sit down for ten minutes if you want.',
    'I was a public defender before I became a priest. I learned most people standing in front of a judge aren\'t bad people. They just ran out of options. I try to be one more option for them.',
    'I\'ll be honest with you, I\'ve lost my faith twice. Once in a courtroom and once next to a hospital bed. Both times it came back the same way, somebody showed up at my door with a casserole. That\'s really all it is. You just show up for people.'],
  sam: ['Hey, don\'t park in front of the bay doors. The truck doesn\'t care that you\'re a cop.',
    'We\'re grilling Thursday. Everybody says they\'ll come and nobody ever does. You should come.',
    'The firefighter who carried me out of my house when I was eight, his name\'s Frank. I tracked him down at a retirement party and walked up and said, "You don\'t know me, but..." Now we do Thanksgiving together every year. His stuffing is terrible. I eat all of it anyway.',
    'Last year I carried a kid out of a fire. He was about the same age I was. Afterward I sat in the truck shaking for an hour, and the guys just left me alone, which is about the nicest thing firefighters know how to do. I keep thinking about how one day that kid might come find me. I hope I\'m still around when he does.'],
  gus: ['Shh, you\'ll scare off the fish. Not that I\'m catching any.',
    'Didn\'t catch anything today. Didn\'t catch anything yesterday either. Still a good couple of days.',
    'My wife used to sit right there and read while I fished. Fifty-one years we did that. Now I bring her books and read them out loud to the river. I like to think the fish enjoy the mysteries.',
    'My doctor says my heart\'s worn out. I told him the river\'s been going a lot longer than me and it\'s doing fine. But when it\'s my time, I want it to be out here with a line in the water and the sun going down, not in some hospital. Somebody should know that. I can\'t tell my kids, they\'d just argue with me.'],
  gerald: ['Hard hat past the yellow line, please. Not my rule.',
    'Count\'s off by one pallet again. It\'s always off by one on Tuesdays. I\'ll find it.',
    'Everybody used to make fun of my clipboard. Then the theft ring happened and the FBI needed to borrow it. I made them sign it out.',
    'My dad couldn\'t read. He worked this port twenty years and memorized everything so nobody would find out. In \'87 they switched to written manifests and he lost his job. So I write everything down. I guess in a way every page I write is for him.'],
  rusty: ['If you\'ve got ID and a receipt, we can talk. No receipt, no copper. Sorry.',
    'Some guy tried to sell me a church bell yesterday. A whole church bell. I called it in before he even finished talking.',
    'People call my yard "the snitch pile." I put it on a T-shirt and sold about forty of them. Mostly to cops, actually. You want one?',
    'I bought stolen stuff one time, back in 1994. Tools somebody took off a widow\'s porch, her husband\'s tools. She came in looking for them and cried right there in my office, and I didn\'t say a word. I\'ve turned down every dirty dollar since then. Thirty years. I can\'t ever give her those tools back.'],
  dee: ['This lot\'s for trucks, honey. Park that cruiser somewhere a trailer won\'t back over it.',
    'You again! Go ahead and check my logs. They\'re perfect, I promise you.',
    'Two point four million miles. You see a lot of people\'s worst days out there. Wrecks, fires, one time a guy hitchhiking through Georgia in a wedding dress. So I wave at everybody. You never know what somebody\'s driving away from.',
    'Diesel was my husband\'s dog. Wendell died on a run outside Amarillo, his heart gave out right there in the cab. That dog sat with him for six hours until the trooper showed up. Diesel rides with me now. He\'s the reason I\'m so careful. I can\'t let that dog lose both of us.'],
  carl: ['We\'re closed after nine. Well, I guess not to you.',
    'Forty-one thousand steps last night. My doctor says I\'m in great shape. My wife says I\'m never home. They\'re both right.',
    'I\'ve stopped three break-ins here and I never had to put my hands on anybody. I was just standing somewhere they didn\'t expect me to be.',
    'Before this I spent fourteen years as a guard at the county jail. I brought that place home with me every night. Here it\'s just boxes and locks. Sometimes I think I took this job so I could watch over things that can\'t get hurt.'],
  shonda: ['Cans go in blue, glass in green. And please, no greasy pizza boxes.',
    'I bet nobody recycles at the station. One of these days I\'m just going to show up with bins.',
    'My mama started this business with a pickup truck and the cans she picked up after church. People called her the garbage lady. That garbage paid for my college. I still drive her truck on Sundays.',
    'Near the end she didn\'t know who I was most days. But if she saw a can on the ground, she\'d pick it up and put it in her pocket. Every single time. Some part of her still knew the job. I wonder sometimes what part of me is going to keep working after the rest forgets.'],
  lupe: ['Menu hasn\'t changed since \'74, so please don\'t ask me for substitutions.',
    'Number four, extra rice, no onions, right? See, I remember.',
    'My grandmother, my mother, and now me, all cooking in the same pots. I scratched my initials next to theirs on the big one. I hope someday somebody scratches theirs next to mine.',
    'I don\'t have any kids. People ask me who\'s going to run the place after I\'m gone, and I don\'t know. Some nights after I lock up, I stand in that kitchen and I can almost hear all three of us in there. Then I think about it being quiet forever and I have to sit down. Anyway. You want some flan?'],
  moore: ['Phones off, hats off, and spit out the gum, please. Thank you.',
    'You stood up when the judge walked in. Most visitors don\'t. I noticed.',
    'Nineteen years and the only famous thing I ever did was say "no" to a guy halfway out the side door. He sat right back down. I\'ve been riding that one ever since. Truth is, I cry at commercials.',
    'The hardest part of this job is the families after a sentencing. Somebody\'s mom, somebody\'s little girl. I\'m supposed to keep order, but mostly I\'m handing out tissues and pretending I\'m not. The county doesn\'t pay for those, by the way. I buy them myself.'],
  barnes: ['If you\'re here to serve papers, wrong office. If you\'re here to ask questions, also wrong office.',
    'Ruiz showed me your report from last month. It was clean. Honestly, I was a little annoyed I had nothing to pick apart.',
    'We\'re not really on opposite sides, you know. You\'re asking whether the government can do something to a person. Your answer should be "maybe." Mine is "prove it."',
    'I lost a trial in 2003 that I should have won. My client did eleven years before the DNA cleared him. Every Christmas he sends me a card thanking me. Thanking me. I can\'t even open them anymore. Ruiz opens them and reads them to me.'],
  ray: ['You need a bond, a lawyer, or directions? I can help with two of those.',
    'One of my clients said you treated him decent on a stop. He doesn\'t say that about anybody.',
    'I bailed my own brother out in \'98 when everybody told me not to. He\'s a pastor down in Mobile now. You take chances on people. Sometimes you lose, but you keep doing it anyway.',
    'I\'ve lost clients. Overdoses, mostly. I go to every single funeral and sit in the back. The families look at me like, who\'s the bail bonds guy? But sometimes I was the last person who still believed in them. Somebody who did ought to be there.'],
  hattie: ['Take a number, please. Yes, you too, officer.',
    'You spelled your own name wrong on a form last week. I fixed it for you. Don\'t do it again.',
    'The first thing I ever stamped was a parking ticket appeal in 1984. The man won. I still have it. Every piece of paper that comes through here is somebody\'s whole day.',
    'One afternoon I stamped my own divorce papers. Same stamp, same desk. Then the next person in line was there for an adoption, so I stamped that too. Life doesn\'t stop for your feelings, honey. You just make sure the date\'s right.'],
  ramos: ['Sally port\'s restricted. Show your badge and wait behind the line.',
    'You searched your back seat before you put him in? Good. You\'d be surprised how many people skip that.',
    'Back in 2001 a guy slipped his cuffs with a pen cap he found in my back seat. I\'m the one who left it there. Nobody got hurt. I just got lucky, and you can\'t count on lucky.',
    'Transport\'s the longest twenty minutes of a lot of people\'s lives. One time a guy asked me to drive past his mom\'s house so he could see her porch light. I couldn\'t take a detour, that\'s policy. But there were two routes the same distance, and I took the one that went down her street. He saw the light. Neither of us said anything.'],
  exhibita: ['(The cat looks at you, then looks away like you\'re not there.)',
    '(The cat lets you stand near her without leaving. That\'s new.)',
    '(The cat drops a rubber band from the clerk\'s office on your boot and stares at you like you should know what that means.)',
    '(The cat climbs into your lap and falls asleep. Clerk Hattie walks by and whispers, "She\'s never done that. Not even for me." She doesn\'t sound happy about it.)'],
  walt: ['Route 9, all stops. Exact change, please.',
    'You should ride along sometime. You\'d be surprised where everybody in this city goes. Mostly the pharmacy.',
    'People keep telling me I was brave during the flood. I really wasn\'t. I just knew the route better than the water did. Mrs. Ochoa called me "mijo" the whole ride, and I\'m sixty-three.',
    'On the seventh trip the water was coming up over the steps. One old man wouldn\'t get on without his wife\'s ashes, so I went back in and got them. I was in water up to my waist. I\'m not saying you have to be brave. I\'m saying sometimes what matters to people isn\'t what you\'d think, and you have to take it seriously anyway.'],
  tasha: ['I\'m retired. Don\'t tell me about your symptoms. ...Okay, fine. Which arm?',
    'You\'re not drinking enough water. I can see it in your face. I did this for thirty years.',
    'The night of the depot fire I treated nineteen people in about two hours. One of them was a young officer named Okonkwo who kept apologizing for bleeding on my shoes. He still sends me flowers every year, and he still apologizes in the card.',
    'The one I think about the most wasn\'t from the fire. It was just a regular Tuesday. An old man, his heart, four in the morning, and nobody came for him. Nobody. So I sat with him until they came to take him, because nobody should have to go alone. If you ever get the chance, sit with them.'],
  leon: ['Floor\'s wet, sorry. Walk on the dry side, I left you a path.',
    'Hey, the kids drew you. Well, a police officer. The arms are really long. I don\'t think they\'ve seen one up close.',
    'I\'ve got about two thousand drawings from the kids over nineteen years. My wife told me to throw some out. I said okay, which ones? She looked through them for an hour and couldn\'t pick a single one. Now she wants more shelves.',
    'When I was a kid, nobody really noticed me. I was the quiet one in the back. But the custodian at my school, Mr. Abernathy, he said "Good morning, Leon" to me every day. That was it. Some days that was the only time anybody said my name. So I learn every one of these kids\' names. Every single one.'],
  carmen: ['Are you hungry? Don\'t lie to me, nobody climbs four floors and isn\'t hungry.',
    'Sit down, sit down. Eat first, then you tell me what\'s wrong. You always look like something\'s wrong.',
    'After María I came here with one suitcase. Some clothes, one picture, and my mother\'s pot. People ask me, "Carmen, only three things?" The pot was the important one. Look how many people that pot feeds now.',
    'My house in Utuado is gone. The whole hillside. I don\'t have any pictures of my kitchen, and that\'s the part I\'m starting to forget first. So I cook it. Every day I cook it the way it smelled in there. If I stop, I\'ll lose it. You understand? I\'m not just feeding people. I\'m trying to remember.'],
  priya: ['Spice goes from one to five. Please don\'t order five as a dare. Please.',
    'Level three again? Good for you. That\'s the smart kind of brave.',
    'I was a bridge engineer for eleven years. I ate lunch by myself at my desk every day, building things that connect people, and I barely talked to anybody. Now I talk to three hundred people a day through this window. Honestly, this is the best bridge I ever built.',
    'My dad told me I was throwing away my degree. He didn\'t talk to me for a whole year. Then one day he\'s just standing in my line like everybody else. He ordered a five and ate the whole thing with tears running down his face. He said it was the spice. He paid full price. He\'s never once said he\'s proud of me. But he comes every Thursday.'],
  hollis: ['You play? No? Sit down anyway. Everybody starts by watching.',
    'Your knight\'s all over the place, cadet. Slow down. Think before you move.',
    'I was locked up nine years, and chess was the only thing in there that made sense. Everybody knows the rules. Nobody lies about what a bishop can do. Out here it\'s harder. You never know what anybody\'s going to do.',
    'I tell every kid who sits down here to think before they move, because I didn\'t. I was nineteen, one night, one bad decision. I\'ve had a long time to think about it since then. You don\'t get that move back. I wish somebody had been sitting here when I was nineteen. So now I sit here.'],
  mo: ['Pump four\'s broken and pump two\'s slow. Coffee\'s free for police, though.',
    'I saved you the last good sandwich. It\'s egg salad. I know, nobody wants egg salad. I saved it anyway.',
    'All the thank-you letters I get from the department, I keep in the back where customers can\'t see them. If people see them, they think I\'m a snitch. But if I don\'t keep them, I forget why I do it.',
    'My father had a station like this back home, and he lost it. He came here and pumped gas for another man for twenty years so I could own one. He died the same month I signed the papers. Every morning when I unlock the door, I say, "Baba, we\'re open."'],
  bea: ['What can I get you? Glazed, chocolate, or the sprinkles that grown men buy "for their kids"?',
    'Five in the morning again, huh? You know you\'re a regular now. No getting out of it.',
    'This is my mother\'s recipe from her bakery in Mexico City. Every December I make one batch exactly the way she did, by hand, all night long, and I give every bit of it away. My knees hate me for it. My mother would have made two.',
    'My day-olds go to the shelter at nine every morning, and they have for twenty years. One day this man in a nice suit buys a dozen and tells me he used to eat my day-olds at the shelter in 2006. He said they were the only thing back then that tasted like somebody cared. I had to go in the back for a minute.'],
  lewis: ['I\'m retired, so please don\'t hand me anything to deliver. My legs will try to do it out of habit.',
    'First it\'s mail piling up on the porch. Then the curtains don\'t move. Then the smell. You learn the order.',
    'Thirty-three years walking a route. You know what a mail carrier really is? The one person who comes by every single house every single day. For some people, I was the only person who came by at all.',
    'There was a lady on Birch Street, Mrs. Gaines, who was writing letters to herself. Took me about a year to notice. She just wanted something in the mailbox with her name on it. So I started writing her. I signed them "a friend from the route." I did that for eleven years. After she passed, her daughter found the letters and came and found me. I still don\'t know what to say about it.'],
  andy: ['Grab a chair. The board\'s free, the coffee isn\'t, and the lessons are free if you don\'t argue with me.',
    'You open the same way every single time. I respect that. I also beat it every time.',
    'That picture by the trophies is my old unit. Gulf War, \'91. Four of us used to play chess in the tent every night. I\'m the only one still playing. Some nights I play their openings. It keeps them in the game, I guess.',
    'When I came home I couldn\'t sit in a room unless I was facing the door. I couldn\'t sleep. Chess was the only thing that slowed my head down to one problem at a time. That\'s really what I\'m teaching these kids. Not chess. Just how to handle one problem at a time.'],
  sunny: ['Oranges are a dollar a bag. Honor box is right there. It works, believe it or not. People are mostly good.',
    'You came back! Here, take a bag. You look like you don\'t eat enough fruit.',
    'Eleven developers have tried to buy this farm. The last one told me, "Everybody has a price." I told him my grandfather\'s price was "the oranges outlive us." He didn\'t know what to say to that.',
    'Honestly, I\'m tired. The farm barely breaks even. Some nights I take that last offer out of the drawer and just read it. Then I walk out into the grove, and the blossoms are out, and it smells exactly like it did when I was six. And I put the offer back in the drawer. Some years that\'s my whole business plan.'],
  jamal: ['Requests go in the jar. Tips go in the jar too. Same jar.',
    'Officer! You coming Saturday? I\'ll play something even you can dance to.',
    'Six years of Saturday nights and not one fight. People think I\'m lucky. It\'s the playlist. When I can feel the crowd getting heated, I put on something their grandma loves. It\'s pretty hard to throw a punch while your aunt\'s singing along.',
    'When I was fifteen, my cousin got shot in a parking lot after a party. I was the DJ that night. Fifteen years old. Ever since then I watch a crowd like my life depends on it. Every Saturday I\'m kind of trying to give that night a better ending.'],
  ines: ['If it bites, please tell me before I\'m holding it.',
    'How\'s the K-9 doing? Tell him I\'ve got a peanut butter treat in the fridge with his name on it.',
    'When a police dog retires, I bake him a peanut butter and pumpkin cake. The handlers cry every single time. These big tough officers. I keep a box of tissues next to the cake now.',
    'The hardest appointment is the last one. The handler sits on the floor with the dog\'s head in his lap. One sergeant stayed four hours and told that dog about every call they ever worked together. I stayed too. The clinic was closed. It didn\'t matter.'],
  pearl: ['Speak up, I\'m ninety-one. Oh, I\'m kidding, I can hear you fine. I just like seeing who\'ll do it.',
    'You\'re the one who always stops and talks to me. People think old means boring. Honey, I\'ve got gossip from 1952.',
    'My house is under that river. They moved the channel in \'49. At low tide you can still see the chimney sticking up. Everybody says it\'s just an old piling. Everybody\'s wrong.',
    'I\'ve outlived two husbands, a son, and every friend I made before 1980. People always ask me what my secret is. There isn\'t one. You just keep showing up for whoever\'s left. Right now that\'s Eli and you. I\'m lucky. I really mean that.'],
  nash: ['Please stay off the infield. I just finished it.',
    'You ever play ball? You\'ve got that catcher stance. Oh wait, no, that\'s just your duty belt.',
    'I played seven years in the minors and never got called up. Now every spring I stripe this field the way they do in the big leagues. It\'s as close as I\'ll ever get. Most days that\'s close enough.',
    'The day I quit, my dad drove four hours to pick me up. He didn\'t say one word the whole drive. Then we stopped for gas and he bought me a Coke and said, "You were good. That\'s not nothing." He\'s been gone ten years now. Every time I finish striping the field, I say it out loud. "That\'s not nothing."'],
  cora: ['Vanilla, chocolate, or blue. Don\'t ask me what blue is, the kids just love it.',
    'You\'ve got the kind of face lost kids walk up to. That\'s a compliment.',
    'Inside the lid of my cart there are forty-one stars. Each one\'s a lost kid I got back to their parents. The first one was in 1998. She teaches second grade now, and she brings her whole class by every May.',
    'I got lost once, at the county fair in 1971. I was six. I sat by the Ferris wheel for half the night until a lady with an ice cream cart found me. That\'s why I have the cart. I\'m not really out here to sell ice cream. I\'m standing where a lost kid would look.'],
  eli: ['You wear a life jacket or you don\'t get a canoe. You can look silly or you can look drowned.',
    'River\'s high today. See that line on the post? Green means don\'t go out. It\'s green.',
    'Twenty years and I\'ve never lost a customer. People think I\'m uptight about the life jackets. Yeah, I am. And I\'m proud of it.',
    'My friend Kenny Ortiz and I were sixteen. He didn\'t wear a life jacket because I didn\'t, because nobody cool wore one. I made it back to shore. He didn\'t. Now I tell every customer, if you won\'t wear it for yourself, wear it for whoever would have to go tell your mom.'],
  toby: ['Nothing for you today, officer. Unless you\'re Mrs. Albright?',
    'You\'re checking porches now, aren\'t you? Mail, curtains, newspapers. Mr. Lewis got to you too.',
    'My first week, Mr. Lewis made me learn every dog\'s name on the route. I said that\'s not the job. He said that\'s exactly the job. He was right. The dogs vouch for you.',
    'Mr. Baptiste on Willow was on his floor for two days before I noticed his mail piling up. He made it, thank God. He told me later he\'d been yelling for help and nobody heard him. Now I knock on his door every day. I don\'t have to. He makes me coffee every time. It\'s terrible coffee. I drink every drop.'],
  tess: ['Yard\'s live. Don\'t cross the tracks unless you want to end up in a training video.',
    'Hear that? That\'s a coupling on track four. You\'ll hear it in your sleep tonight. Everybody does.',
    'My grandmother threw these same switches in 1943. Her initials are carved into the window frame right there. E.R. I touch them every time I start a shift.',
    'She told me that during the war she\'d watch the troop trains go by and say a prayer for every car. She didn\'t know anybody on them. She just thought somebody should. I do it now for the freight. Lumber, grain, cars. It probably sounds dumb. But every one of those cars is going to somebody.'],
  hector: ['Sparks fly far. Stay behind the line or you\'ll lose your eyebrows.',
    'Want to try it? Mask down, and don\'t look at the light. Everybody looks at the light.',
    'I\'ve had over a hundred kids come through this garage. One of them welded the new flagpole at Station 3. I take the long way to work just to drive past it. Don\'t tell Lupe. She thinks I take the long way for her.',
    'My own son went down the wrong road and I couldn\'t reach him. Prison, then he got out, and now I don\'t even know where he is. So every kid who walks in here, I try to teach like he\'s my son. I couldn\'t be a good father to one. I\'m trying to make up for it with a hundred. I know it doesn\'t work that way.'],
  ivy: ['Move slow around the hives. They don\'t hate you, they\'re just busy.',
    'Here, try this. That\'s clover. Next week it\'ll taste like orange blossom. Same bees, different week.',
    'I started keeping bees because my son had bad allergies and local honey was supposed to help. He grew out of them by twelve. I never grew out of the bees. Funny how you start something for somebody else and end up keeping it for yourself.',
    'He lives far away now. He calls when he remembers, which isn\'t very often. But every evening, I get to watch forty thousand bees come home. They always come home. I know it\'s not the same thing. Some evenings it\'s enough.'],
  wanda: ['Shh. There\'s a painted bunting right... and it\'s gone. Thanks.',
    'You\'re on my bird list now, you know. "Officer, common. Startles easily."',
    'Everybody says I was so brave to report that man burying a safe in \'97. Honestly, I was mostly annoyed. He scared off a woodpecker I\'d been waiting on all morning.',
    'I started bird watching after my husband left. I needed some reason to get up in the morning besides waiting by the phone. And if you sit still long enough, things come to you. Birds, mostly. Some people too. Hector. You.'],
  pat: ['This is freight dispatch. Unless you\'re a truck, I probably can\'t help you.',
    'Your channel bleeds through on mine sometimes. Your dispatcher\'s good. Velvet. I\'d know that voice anywhere.',
    'Velvet and I have never actually met. Thirty years. We talk on the phone every Christmas Eve. We tell each other our worst calls of the year first and then the funniest ones. We always end on the funny ones.',
    'I asked her once if we should finally meet in person. She said, "Pat, if we meet, we\'re just two old ladies. On the phone we\'re legends." So we never have. That\'s radio for you. You feel closer to people than you really are.'],
  lou: ['Bay\'s hot. Don\'t touch anything orange.',
    'Had a truck in today, the brake pads were basically gone. My jar of bad bolts is just about full.',
    'I don\'t really care about trucks. I care about some kid not getting run over because some guy couldn\'t afford new brakes.',
    'My brother drove trucks. He had a blowout on a bad tire, and it came out later the company had skipped the inspection. I was twenty. I got my certification the next year. Every truck that leaves this bay, I sign my own name on it. Not the shop\'s name, mine. If something happens, it\'s on me.'],
  alvarez: ['I\'m retired, so please don\'t call me "Your Honor." But do tell me if you see my reading glasses.',
    'The first time you testify, your hands shake. By the fifth time, it\'s your voice. By the twentieth, nothing shakes, and that\'s when you need to be careful.',
    'I threw out one of Castillo\'s cases in \'95 because his report didn\'t match his testimony. He was furious with me. Ten years later he sent me a note that said, "Best lesson I ever got." I kept it in my robe pocket. The robe\'s in a closet now, but the note\'s still in it.',
    'In 1990 I sentenced a nineteen-year-old to twenty years because the law said I had to, and I didn\'t fight it. He got out last spring. I went to the gate and told him I was sorry. He looked at me and said, "For what?" And I couldn\'t answer him. I still can\'t.'],
  nico: ['What can I get you? We\'ve got the Objection Roast, the Sustained Blend, and the decaf we call the Mistrial.',
    'Double shot? You look like you testified this morning. Did you? I can always tell.',
    'I\'m first in my class at night school. I study between rushes. Half the judges give me legal advice when they tip. Mostly about parking tickets.',
    'My dad notarized papers in this building for thirty years for people who couldn\'t afford a lawyer. When I pass the bar, I want him to be the one to stamp my oath. They keep telling me that\'s not how it works. I\'m going to ask anyway.'],
  greer: ['Please speak clearly and don\'t talk over each other. That\'s all I ever ask.',
    'You say "um" about four times a minute on the stand. Sorry, I count. It\'s a habit.',
    'Eleven hundred trials. The funny thing is, I remember the words but not the faces. I could recite a defendant\'s sentence and walk right past him in the grocery store.',
    'Some testimony I typed with my eyes closed because I couldn\'t look at the person saying it. My fingers just kept going. That\'s the job, you don\'t stop the record because it hurts. When I get home, my husband reads me the comics out loud. Every night for twenty-eight years.'],
  felix: ['Two forms of ID, and you sign it in front of me, not before. Sorry. It\'s the law.',
    'You wouldn\'t believe what people want notarized. Yesterday it was an adoption agreement for a goldfish. It was legal, too.',
    'I caught that forged deed because the woman who supposedly signed it had died the year before. I knew because I\'d notarized her will. You remember the people who sit in that chair.',
    'One of the widows from that fraud ring got her house back, and she came in and asked me to notarize a thank-you card. It wasn\'t a legal document or anything, just a card. I stamped it anyway. Best thing I\'ve ever put my seal on.'],
  ada: ['Records Annex. If it happened in this county, it\'s in a box down here somewhere. Maybe even the right box.',
    'You search like a detective. Bottom shelves first. Nobody ever checks the bottom shelves.',
    'I found the city\'s founding charter while I was looking for a parking ticket. That\'s how archives work. You never find what you\'re looking for, but you find what\'s been waiting for you.',
    'I keep a box for people nobody ever came to claim. Unclaimed estates, old photos, letters. I index them anyway. Their name, their dates, and one line about who they were if I can figure it out. Somebody should be able to find them someday. Even if nobody ever does.'],
  booker: ['Take your hat off near the wall, please.',
    'You stopped and read one of the names last time. Most people walk right past. I notice who stops.',
    'I knew three of the men on that wall. I polish their names last, and I take my time with them. Davis would laugh at me for that.',
    'I was supposed to be on that patrol. I traded with Davis because I had a toothache. A toothache. Fifty-some years later and that\'s still the stupidest, biggest thing that ever happened to me. So I keep this wall clean. It\'s the only shift I can still cover for him.']
};
export const HEART_TIER = (f) => (f >= 6 ? 3 : f >= 3 ? 2 : f >= 1 ? 1 : 0);

